Q: Why is this expression treated as a first member value rather than a structure value? A: <p>The first value in the curly initializer (<code>*a</code>, a <code>struct load_con</code>) is used to initialize the first field (<code>void* X</code>). They're not compatible.</p>
<p>You can use</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>a = ( load_con[] ){ *a };
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>a = &amp;( load_con ){ a-&gt;X, a-&gt;Y };
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>load_con lc = *a;
a = &amp;lc;
</code></pre>

Q: How to escape new lines in rendered message using Serilog and Console sink? A: <p>I got this question answered at <a href="https://github.com/serilog/serilog/issues/2059" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/serilog/serilog/issues/2059</a>. In the nutshell, here is what you need to do (assuming you are using <code>Serilog.Expressions</code>):</p>
<p>Create a method to escape new lines:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class MyFunctions
{
    // This is needed for appsettings configuration.
    public static NameResolver NameResolver 
    { 
        get; 
    } = new StaticMemberNameResolver(typeof(MyFunctions));

    // Function that escapes new line characters.
    public static LogEventPropertyValue? EscapeNewLines
    (
        StringComparison comparison,
        LogEventPropertyValue? maybeMessage
    )
    {
        string? message = null;

        if (maybeMessage != null)
        {
            if (maybeMessage is ScalarValue sv)
            {
                if (sv.Value is string s)
                {
                    message = s;
                }
                else if (sv.Value != null)
                {
                    message = sv.Value?.ToString();

                    if (message != null &amp;&amp; message == sv.Value?.GetType().FullName)
                    {
                        message = sv.Value.ToJson();
                    }
                }
            }
            else
            {
                message = maybeMessage.ToString();
            }
        }

        return new ScalarValue(message?.Replace(&quot;\r&quot;, &quot;\\r&quot;).Replace(&quot;\n&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In the expression template, call this method on the message and exception:</p>
<pre><code>{EscapeNewlines(@m)} ... {EscapeNewlines(@x)}
</code></pre>
<p>In the bootstrap logger initialization, register the name resolver as:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>.WriteTo.Console(new ExpressionTemplate(
    // Expression template
    &quot;... {EscapeNewlines(@m)} ... {EscapeNewlines(@x)}&quot;,
    // Your name resolver
    nameResolver: new StaticMemberNameResolver(typeof(MyFunctions)))
</code></pre>
<p>In the app settings file, also register the name resolver:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;formatter&quot;: {
  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Serilog.Templates.ExpressionTemplate, Serilog.Expressions&quot;,
  &quot;template&quot;: &quot;... {EscapeNewlines(@m)} ... {EscapeNewlines(@x)}&quot;,
  &quot;nameResolver&quot;: &quot;MyNamespace.MyFunctions::NameResolver, MyAssembly&quot;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to overload operator&lt;&lt; for a C-style array passed by reference to a function? A: <p>I would have a wrapper function that returns an object that knows how to print it.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct ArrayPrinter
{
    T*          array;
    std::size_t size;

    ArrayPrinter(T* array, std::size_t size)
        : array(array)
        , size(size)
    {}

    friend std::ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(std::ostream&amp; stream, ArrayPrinter const&amp; data)
    {
        os &lt;&lt; std::hex;

        for (std::size_t loop = 0; loop &lt; data.size; ++loop) {
            os &lt;&lt; std::string(&quot;0x&quot;) 
               &lt;&lt; std::setw(2)
               &lt;&lt; std::setfill('0')
               &lt;&lt; data.array[loop]
               &lt;&lt; std::string(&quot; &quot;);
        }
        os &lt;&lt; std::dec;
        return os;
    }
};

template&lt;typename T, std::size_t Size&gt;
ArrayPrinter&lt;T&gt; arrayPrinter(T (&amp;array)[Size])
{
    return ArrayPrinter&lt;T&gt;{array, Size};
}

int main()
{
    int data[] = {1,2,3,4,5, 67};

    std::cout &lt;&lt; arrayPrinter(data) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
